[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of the TISSUENARRATOR framework. A. TISSUENARRATOR takes as input a tissue patch, encodes individual cells into cell sentences, and assembles a spatial sentence from neighboring cells. The spatial sentence can be interpreted by an LLM with biological prior knowledge and contextual understanding to predict gene expressions in a cell and to answer questions about tissues. Text output of the LLM can be further transformed into gene expression levels. B. TISSUENARRATOR supports four downstream tasks: cell generation, neighborhood perturbation, genetic perturbation, and conversational tissue querying. For cell generation tasks, model-generated cell sentences can be converted back into gene expression space.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific figure with two labeled panels, **A** and **B**, describing an LLM-based framework for spatial/tissue data representation and prediction.\n\n**Panel A: Workflow**\n- Starts from a **tissue patch** shown as a small cluster of stylized cells, with a zoomed region indicated from a brain-like tissue icon.\n- The tissue patch is converted into **cell sentences**, shown as an **ordered gene list** per cell, with example gene tokens such as `G1, G2, G3, ...`, `G2, G1, G3, ...`, and `G4, G2, G5, ...`.\n- These cell sentences are then transformed into a **spatial sentence**, represented as an **ordered cell list**.\n- Each cell entry includes structured fields: `<x, y>` position, `cell type`, metadata, and gene list.\n- An **LLM** is shown consuming the spatial sentence, with annotations for **biological prior knowledge** and **contextual understanding**.\n- Outputs are split into:\n  - **Generative prediction**, including:\n    - **Cell prediction**, where a missing or unknown cell representation is predicted.\n    - **Text prediction**, with example text: “The major cell types in this region are microglia, astrocytes, ________”\n  - **Quantitative prediction**, showing a table predicting gene expression values for genes `G1`, `G2`, `G3`, with example values `.9`, `.6`, `.2`.\n\n**Panel B: Applications**\nShows four application boxes:\n- **Cell generation**: generating cells conditioned on optional metadata, including type.\n- **Conversational tissue querying**: a brain/tissue icon connected to chat bubbles and a user icon, implying question-answer interaction over tissue data.\n- **Genetic perturbation**: altered gene expression in a highlighted cell or cell group.\n- **Neighborhood perturbation**: altered neighborhood composition among nearby cells.\n\n**Biological source objects**\n- Tissue patch / brain-region context.\n- Individual cells with different colors and shapes.\n- Cell types including explicitly mentioned **microglia** and **astrocytes**.\n- Gene lists and gene expression values.\n\n**Model interface and transformations**\n- Biological tissue/spatial cell data are serialized into text-like “sentences.”\n- Cell-level gene lists become ordered cell representations with position, type, metadata, and genes.\n- These structured spatial sentences are passed to an LLM for generative, textual, and quantitative prediction tasks.\n\n**Findings or claim conveyed**\nThe figure proposes that tissue patches can be encoded as structured spatial sentences for LLM-based modeling, enabling cell prediction, text prediction, gene-expression prediction, cell generation, tissue querying, and perturbation analysis."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Figure 2: Cell-cell interaction modeling in mouse brain MERFISH data. A. In situ plot of annotated cell types in a representative slide. B. Left: pericytes (Peri; red) are designated as proximal and control depending on their distance to the closest border-associated macrophage (BAM; blue). Top-right: correlation of log-fold changes between generated proximal vs control and ground-truth proximal vs control comparisons. Bottomright: ligand-receptor pathways upregulated in proximal vs control cell pairs. C. Same as B, but for olfactory astrocytes (Astro-OLF; red) and inhibitory immature neurons (Inh IMN; blue). D. Generated expression of Sfrp1 , a gene previously reported to be upregulated in Astro-OLF cells proximal to Inh IMN cells. E-F. NDCG (E) and Overlap Score (F) comparing model-generated and ground-truth cell sentences under two evaluation settings. The left panels show the conditioned generation results, and the right panels show the unconditioned generation results. '@k' indicates evaluation using the top 10, 30, or 50 genes. TN, TissueNarrator; NF , Nicheformer; CM, Class Mean; NN, Nearest Neighbor; C2S, C2S-Scale.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F.\n\nPanel A shows a spatial map or tissue section with many colored points over a brain-like anatomical slice. A legend lists many cell-type classes, including Astro-Epen, CB Glut, multiple GABA/Glut neuronal groups, immune, vascular, OPC-Oligo, OEC, TH Glut, and others. The biological source object appears to be brain tissue with spatially resolved cell annotations.\n\nPanels B and C show schematic spatial neighborhood examples at left and quantitative ligand-receptor/model outputs at right.\n\nPanel B is labeled “Peri - BAM”. The left plot shows gray background cells with highlighted red and blue cells, plus dashed boxed regions labeled “Proximal” and “Control.” The right side includes a scatter plot comparing “LFC Predicted” versus “LFC GT,” and a ligand-receptor fold-change plot with LAMININ and COLLAGEN.\n\nPanel C is labeled “Astro-OLF - Inh IMN”. The left plot similarly shows spatial cells with dashed “Proximal” and “Control” regions. The right side includes a “LFC Predicted” versus “LFC GT” scatter plot and a ligand-receptor fold-change plot with ncWNT and EPHA.\n\nPanel D is a boxplot titled “Sfrp1,” comparing Expression between “control” and “proximal.” Expression is near zero in control and elevated in proximal.\n\nPanel E contains bar charts of NDCG for “Conditioned Generation” and “Unconditioned Generation,” with bars at @10, @30, and @50. Methods/categories on the x-axis include TN, NF, CM, NN, Base, and C2S.\n\nPanel F contains similar grouped bar charts for “Overlap,” again comparing conditioned and unconditioned generation across TN, NF, CM, NN, Base, and C2S at @10, @30, and @50.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to evaluate spatial cell-cell interaction or ligand-receptor prediction models using spatial transcriptomics/cell-type maps, proximal versus control neighborhoods, log fold-change prediction accuracy, ligand-receptor enrichment, Sfrp1 expression differences, and retrieval/generation metrics such as NDCG and overlap."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 3: Modeling spatial and genetic perturbations in silico . A. Scatter plot of the in silico cross-regional transplant experiment. A thalamus glutamatergic neuron (TH Glut; red) from one section is transplanted into another section, near olfactory ensheathing cells (OEC; blue) B. GSEA results for OEC cells proximal to the transplanted TH Glut cell, showing downregulation of ECM pathways. C. Visualization of melanoma tumor cells from the Perturb-FISH dataset, including 35 gene perturbations. Each perturbation is shown in a unique color, while grey cells represent unassigned cases. D. Comparison of log-fold changes between generated and ground-truth differential expression for proximal knockout vs control neighbors. Knock-out of CHUK and MAP2K2 are shown as examples.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with labeled panels A-D.\n\nPanel A shows a schematic question: “How a cell responds to perturbed neighbor composition.” A cartoon person icon points to a boxed diagram of blue cell-like objects surrounding a dashed central cell with a question mark. To the right is a UMAP/spatial-style embedding titled “In-silico Cross-Regional Transplant,” with colored cell populations and an arrow indicating transplantation or transfer. Legend labels: Other in light gray, TH Glut in red, OEC in blue.\n\nPanel B shows a horizontal dot/line enrichment plot with x-axis “NES.” Terms listed on the y-axis include “Gene Expression (Transcription),” “Hemostasis,” and “Extracellular Matrix Organization.” Blue points indicate NES values; Gene Expression appears positive, while Hemostasis and Extracellular Matrix Organization appear negative.\n\nPanel C shows another schematic question: “How do neighbors respond to a gene knockout?” A cartoon person icon points to a boxed diagram with a purple central perturbed/knockout-like object and dashed neighboring cell outlines with question marks. To the right is a spatial tissue-like map titled “Neighbor Generation (Unseen KO),” showing two tissue sections filled with many colored predicted/generated neighbor points over gray background tissue.\n\nPanel D shows two scatter plots comparing generated and measured perturbation effects. Left plot title: “CHUK: Spearman ρ=0.51.” Right plot title: “MAP2K2: Spearman ρ=0.63.” Both plots have y-axis “Generated LFC” and x-axis “Ground-truth LFC,” with dashed horizontal/vertical zero reference lines and blue scatter points.\n\nBiological objects visible: cells, cell neighborhoods, tissue/spatial transcriptomics-like maps, gene knockouts, gene expression/transcription signatures, and specific genes CHUK and MAP2K2.\n\nModel/interface concepts visible: in-silico cross-regional transplant, neighbor generation for unseen knockout perturbations, comparison of generated log fold changes against ground-truth log fold changes.\n\nMain findings shown visually: generated neighbor responses correlate with ground truth for CHUK and MAP2K2 with Spearman correlations of 0.51 and 0.63, respectively; pathway/gene-set NES changes are summarized for transcription, hemostasis, and extracellular matrix organization."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Fig. 2. Ovarian cancer data analysis. A. Spatial distribution of cell types in a representative sample SMI_T10_F001. B. Illustration of the tumor infiltration program, showing genes that are differentially expressed in immune cells as a function of their proximity to malignant cells within the tumor region. C. Area under the ROC curve (AUC) scores indicate that the model distinguishes between immune cell types rather than collapsing into a mixed immune population. D. Differential expression analysis of model-generated CD8 T cells. Gene rankings derived from model predictions are linearly mapped to expression levels. Significantly differentially expressed genes that align with those reported in the original study are highlighted in the volcano plot on the left, and GSEA results are shown on the right. E. Same analysis as in D, but for monocytes. Figure 4: Modeling immune-tumor interactions in ovarian cancer. A. Spatial distribution of cell types in a representative sample (SMI_T10_F001). B. Illustration of the tumor infiltration program, showing genes that are differentially expressed in immune cells as a function of their proximity to malignant cells within the tumor region. C. Area under the ROC curve (AUC) scores computed from the expression of reported marker genes for each immune subtype. D. Differential expression of model-generated CD8 + T cells proximal vs non-proximal to malignant cells. Significantly differentially expressed genes that align with those reported in the original study are highlighted in the volcano plot on the left (thresholds: | logFC | ≥ 0 . 5 , FDR ≤ 0 . 001 ), and GSEA results are shown on the right. E. Same analysis as in D, but for monocytes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled A-E.\n\nPanel A: Spatial tissue/cell map with points colored by inferred cell type. Legend lists B.cell, Endothelial, Fibroblast, Malignant, Mast.cell, Monocyte, and TNK.cell. Red malignant cells dominate the outer tissue regions, with green fibroblast-like cells and other immune/stromal classes distributed through the central area.\n\nPanel B: Schematic cartoon labeled “Tumor Region” and “Tumor Infiltration program,” showing immune/stromal cells moving toward or infiltrating a cluster of tumor cells.\n\nPanel C: Horizontal dot/interval plot of AUC values for cell types Treg, NK.cell, CD8.T.cell, CD4.T.cell, Monocyte, and B.cell. The x-axis is AUC, approximately 0.5 to 0.8.\n\nPanel D: Volcano plot comparing gene expression with x-axis logFC and y-axis -logFDR. Points are colored as not significant, down-regulated, and up-regulated. Labeled genes include CD44, SPP1, SPPB1, CXCR4, CD28, and CTSW. To the right is a pathway enrichment dot plot with NES on the x-axis and color indicating -log10(p.adjust), showing pathways including Cellular Responses to Stress, Costimulation by the CD28 Family, Metabolism of Proteins, Cellular Responses to Stimuli, and Adaptive Immune System.\n\nPanel E: Second volcano plot with logFC and -logFDR axes, similarly colored by regulation status. Labeled genes include CLEC10A, CFD, CCL18, LY7, FCER1G, SRGN, and CLEC5A. To the right is another pathway enrichment dot plot with NES and -log10(p.adjust), including Cellular Responses to Stress, Metabolism of Proteins, Cellular Responses to Stimuli, Response to Elevated Platelet Cytosolic Ca2+, Platelet Degranulation, Post-translational Protein Modification, Platelet Activation/Signaling/Aggregation, Nervous System Development, Axon Guidance, and Developmental Biology.\n\nVisible finding: the figure links spatial tumor infiltration/cell-type patterns with differential gene expression and pathway enrichment, highlighting immune/stromal tumor-region programs and associated stress, immune, protein metabolism, and platelet-related pathways."]}]

